- A `global using` alias exists only inside its assembly. A public signature that uses it shows the real type to apps; decide the public name when the API is public.
- Code that treats layers differently by how they are drawn tells them apart by their plan's type, not their spec's: a split layer (`SplitPlan`) and a stat layer (`StatPlan`) keep the spec of the geom they draw. PR #275's `AutoRangeY` chose layers by `LineLayerSpec` and read a split line's column with a search over the whole column, whose x starts again in each run.

## Errors

- Argument and state checks throw through `ThrowHelper` (`.cursor/rules/perf.mdc`), with one-sentence English messages that name the condition.
- An internal invariant violation calls `Invariant.Fail` (debug: throws `InvariantViolationException`, so a test can observe it; release: `Environment.FailFast`, design §0.1). Never `Debug.Fail`: without a debugger attached it ends the process. It is not caught.
- Public exception types are few and documented in the XML docs of the members that throw them. A public member lists what its internal callees throw too: compare each internal member's `<exception>` lines with every public member that reaches it. In PR #108, `TextEngine.Prepare` threw for a glyph larger than a page, and the review found it missing from `IGpuBackend.Draw`.
- A public type that wraps its arguments into another type's (a width into a `RenderTargetDescription`) checks them first, so that `ArgumentException.ParamName` is the caller's name. PR #39's `HeadlessRenderer(backend, 0, 100)` first threw for `description`. A check that two entry points call (a factory and the compile of the same value) takes the caller's parameter name as an argument: PR #259's checks of a figure's cells named `grid` and `plots`, which neither `Figure.Grid(…, cells)` nor `Render(figure)` has.
- A `catch` that runs cleanup which can throw keeps the exception it caught: when the cleanup throws too, throw an `AggregateException` with the caught exception first, then the cleanup's. The rule holds in benchmarks and tools too (a `Process.Kill` in a `catch`).
- A comment that states how a runtime or OS API behaves names what was checked: the call, the version, and the result. What the documentation says and what a run showed are written apart.
- A call that can fail by its result is checked: `Matrix3x2.Invert` returns `false` for a singular matrix and fills the result with NaN. A singular transform is a normal input (a zoom to a range of 0, an axis with one distinct value).
- Work counted across threads counts the threads that fail to start: a `Thread.Start` that throws leaves its share undone, and a wait for the count never returns. Do that share on the calling thread.
- A rule that two stages read (whether a facet's x is free, which the plan learns by and the guides choose ticks by) is one member both call, not a condition written in each. PR #247 wrote "free x" as the facet's `FreeX` in the plan and as `FreeX` and not a band in the guides; a band x with a secondary x learned one way and laid out the other. `FacetArrangement.FreeXOf` is now the one rule.
- One quantity computed in two places and compared (a bucket's bounds from the pyramid and from the raw data) is computed the same way in both, or compared with a tolerance whose size and reason are written beside it. The tolerance goes into every comparison of that quantity, and of two stages in a row, the later accepts twice the tolerance, so that their bounds do not meet.
- A check that runs in Debug builds only does not change the state it checks: a check that reads through a cache must not fill the cache, or Debug and Release differ in memory and in what they compute.
- A static value that threads share and that is made when first read uses `Lazy<T>` with `LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication`, not `??=`. With `??=`, two threads (a worker and the UI thread) can each make one, each with an id of its own.
- A check on a combination of a positional record's members runs where the value is taken (a setter, a constructor's parameter), not in the record. `with` copies the value and sets members through `init`, and never calls the primary constructor, so a check there does not see `value with { ... }`; a check in `init` sees one member set before the other and refuses a valid result.

- A relative tolerance in a check is relative to the magnitude of what is compared as a whole (a range's larger end), not to a value that can be 0. PR #235 checked a transform's round trip relative to each end of the domain; at an end of 0 the tolerance was about 0, and a monotonic transform that offsets and scales was refused. The review found it.

## XML docs

- Public members under `src/` have `summary`, and `param` / `returns` / `exception` when a caller might be unsure. Sentences end with a period. Properties start with "Gets" / "Gets or sets"; constructors with "Initializes a new instance of the <see cref="T"/> class".
- XML docs state the caller's contract only. How it works lives in `docs/design.ja.md`; refer to the section instead of restating it.
- A commit that changes a contract also searches the XML docs for the old contract's words and rewrites what they say in the same push. PR #19 changed what a provisional answer reads and when the pool sets a fault, and its review found five summaries and remarks that still said "answered from its chunk statistics" and "pyramids with chunks left".
- Use `<inheritdoc />` when an implementation has the interface's contract.
- A member put in before another goes above the other's XML doc comment, not between the comment and the member. A script that anchors on the member's signature splits them: PR #379 put `UnionOf` under `Union`'s `<summary>`, so that one method had two and the other none, and the build did not object.
- A member that returns one shared instance of a public `IDisposable` says in its XML docs that callers do not dispose it.
- Wording and terminology: `.agents/skills/english-writing/SKILL.md`.
